def eurorack-power
  title "Eurorack power entry: keyed 10-pin header, series Schottky per rail, 10 µF per rail"
  about "The ±12 V of a Eurorack bus through a shrouded 2 × 5 box header (the key lets a keyed IDC socket seat one way
      only), an SS14 Schottky in series with each rail – a reversed ribbon swaps +12 V and −12 V, and both diodes
      block – and a 10 µF electrolytic per rail behind its diode. Pin 1 (red stripe) and 2 carry −12 V, 3–8 ground, 9
      and 10 +12 V: the 10-pin subset of the Doepfer A-100 bus."
  port p12 "the protected +12 V rail of the module"
  port n12 "the protected −12 V rail of the module"
  port gnd "ground"
  part J1 connector BH254V-10P lcsc C492442 source reversible "{as}: Eurorack power 10-pin (pin 1 = red stripe = −12
      V)"
    notes "Pins 1/2 −12 V (red stripe), 3–8 GND, 9/10 +12 V. Print '−12V' or 'red stripe' beside pin 1 on the
      silkscreen."
  part D1 diode SS14 lcsc C2480 "{as}: +12 V series Schottky"
    notes "Anode at the header: a reversed ribbon puts −12 V there and D1 blocks. 40 V VRRM; edakit holds the 25.2 V
      between the nets' ranges (header −12.6 V, rail +12.6 V) × 1.5 = 37.8 V to it. 1 A; up to 0.55 V drop at 1 A."
  part D2 diode SS14 lcsc C2480 "{as}: −12 V series Schottky"
    notes "Cathode at the header: the rail's current flows from the module to the bus; a reversed ribbon puts +12 V
      there and D2 blocks."
  part C1 capacitor RVT10UF35V67RV0176 lcsc C2895284 value 10uF "{as}: +12 V rail 10 µF"
    notes "Plus (pin 1) at +12 V. 35 V: room above 12.6 V × 1.5 = 18.9 V for plug-in transients on the ribbon."
  part C2 capacitor RVT10UF35V67RV0176 lcsc C2895284 value 10uF "{as}: −12 V rail 10 µF"
    notes "Plus (pin 1) at ground, minus at −12 V."
  net p12_bus -12.6..12.6 V J1.9 J1.10 D1.anode
    notes "+12 V at the header; −12 V with a reversed ribbon."
  net n12_bus -12.6..12.6 V J1.1 J1.2 D2.cathode
    notes "−12 V at the header (red stripe); +12 V with a reversed ribbon."
  net p12 0..12.6 V D1.cathode C1.plus
  net n12 -12.6..0 V D2.anode C2.minus
  net gnd J1.3 J1.4 J1.5 J1.6 J1.7 J1.8 C1.minus C2.plus
  covers PATH.reverse LIM.v
  notes "Pinout from the Doepfer A-100 bus as the open designs DIYSynthMNL/EuroBusboard (README, 10-pin IDC) and
      hed0rah/partinfo (eurorack-power.json, after Noise Engineering's diagram) give it – doepfer.de was not reachable
      from here; check it against the A-100 bus specification before an order."
  notes "Rail ranges ±12.6 V: ±5 % as an assumption for a regulated Eurorack supply, not from the A-100 specification
      – set the nets to the case's power supply. The header nets span both polarities (a reversed ribbon)."
  notes "The keyed header protects only with a keyed IDC socket on a cable crimped the same way at both ends; the
      diodes stay for unshrouded bus boards and cables made the wrong way round. The modules' +12 V and −12 V lose the
      diode's drop (up to 0.55 V at 1 A)."
  notes "Not covered: a 16-pin bus cable plugged one row offset onto a 16-pin header puts +12 V or +5 V on the
      module's ground pins – the diodes cannot stop that; the 2 × 5 shroud keeps a 16-pin socket out. For +5 V from
      the bus (16-pin, pins 11/12) the 2 × 8 header with a third diode."
  notes "Graph: J1 carries \"source\": \"reversible\" – PATH.reverse checks that a reversed ribbon reaches only the
      diodes. It reports D2's cathode on n12_bus: paths.toml knows a blocking diode only with its anode at the source
      (a positive rail); on the negative rail the cathode faces the header and blocks the reversed +12 V – the block
      accepts that finding on n12_bus. Nothing else belongs on the header side of D1 and D2."
  notes "10 µF per rail (maker-coverage BL-15, a design choice – not from the A-100 specification) behind the diode,
      so that a reversed ribbon never reverse-biases the electrolytic; 100 nF at each IC's supply pins (REQ.missing).
      Optional: 10 Ω or a ferrite per rail ahead of the capacitor, an RC filter against what other modules put on the
      bus (the pattern hed0rah/partinfo's eurorack-power-protection describes)."
  notes "sim.json: J1 as one source with two rails – \"J1\": {\"minus\": \"3\", \"outputs\": {\"p12\": {\"plus\":
      \"9\", \"v_ratio\": 1}, \"n12\": {\"plus\": \"1\", \"v_ratio\": -1}}} and the supply \"J1\": 12 (handbook 5.1);
      each rail's current is its own row (J1:p12, J1:n12)."
  set test {
     "connect": {
      "p12": "+12V",
      "n12": "-12V"
     },
     "nets": {
      "+12V": [
       0,
       12.6
      ],
      "-12V": [
       -12.6,
       0
      ]
     },
     "sim": {
      "sources": {
       "J2": {
        "minus": "3",
        "r_ohm": 0.1,
        "outputs": {
         "p12": {
          "plus": "9",
          "v_ratio": 1
         },
         "n12": {
          "plus": "1",
          "v_ratio": -1
         }
        },
        "note": "the bus board's ±12 V on the header that becomes J2 in the example: one source, two rails against GND (pin 3) – values for the test, not a data sheet's"
       }
      },
      "supplies": {
       "usb-min": {
        "J1": 4.75,
        "J2": 11.4
       },
       "usb-nom": {
        "J1": 5.0,
        "J2": 12.0
       },
       "usb-max": {
        "J1": 5.25,
        "J2": 12.6
       }
      }
     }
     }
